                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesApplications"} Applications
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The DHR superselection theory is about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superselection+sectors"} superselection sectors
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+approach"} Haag-Kastler approach
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+net"} Haag-Kastler net
            that specify the representations of the quasi-local algebra that are deemed physically admissible.
          %p
            It is named after
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergio+Doplicher"} Sergio Doplicher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rudolf+Haag"} Rudolf Haag
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Roberts"} John Roberts
          %p
            In the following we consider the theory starting with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+vacuum+representation"} Haag-Kastler vacuum representation
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
          %p
            The DHR condition says that
            %strong all expectation values (of all observables) should approach the vacuum expectation values, uniformly, when the region of measurement is moved away from the origin.
            This is one possible abstraction of the situation in a high energy particle collider, where the registered events are localized both in time and in space.
          %p
            The DHR condition excludes long range forces like electromagnetism from consideration, because, by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes%27+theorem"} Stokes' theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Doplicher-Roberts+reconstruction+theorem"} Doplicher-Roberts reconstruction theorem
          %h2#abstract Abstract
          %p
            After the definition of admissible representations we collect some notions that will enable us to state a description of all admissible representations using intrinsic properties of the quasi-local algebra. The central concept that we define is that of a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong transportable endomorphism
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/DHR+category"} DHR category
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            We start with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+vacuum+representation"} Haag-Kastler vacuum representation
            that we assume to be irreducible and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag+dual"} Haag dual
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
            be the vacuum representation from now on,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            denote double cones and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒥
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_0
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+index+set"} causal index set
            of double cones, just as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            are bounded open sets and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+index+set"} causal index set
            of bounded open sets.
          %p
            Recall that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi clo
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒥
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{A} := clo_{\| \cdot \|} \bigl( \bigcup_{\mathcal{O}\in\mathcal{J}}\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) \bigr)
          %p
            is called the quasi-local algebra of the given net. We will in the same way associate a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            to every open region
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_0
            via
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi clo
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ⊇
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒥
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_0) := clo_{\| \cdot \|} \bigl( \bigcup_{\mathcal{O}_0 \supseteq \mathcal{O}\in\mathcal{J}}\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) \bigr)
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A representation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              of the local algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              is called (DHR)
              %strong admissible
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒦
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi | \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{K}^{\perp})
              is unitarily equivalent to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒦
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0 | \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{K}^{\perp})
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒥
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \in \mathcal{J}_0
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p
            Note that the equivalence of the representations is required for the causal complement of
            %em all
            double cones, not a special one, so that the characterization that “the representations can not be distinguished at space-like infinity” is misleading.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p Recall that a mapping
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\rho: \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{A}
            %p
              is called a
              %strong unital endomorphism
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              is linear, multiplicative (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi AB
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(AB) = \rho(A) \rho(B)
              ) and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝟙
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(\mathbb{1})=1
          %p We will drop “unitarily” from now on.
          #RepresentationsFromEndomorphismsAndViceVersa.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              For any representation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              and endomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              the composition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \circ \rho
              is another representation.
            %p
              Conversely, under the assumption of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag+duality"} Haag duality
              every DHR admissible representation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              comes from an endomorphism this way. For let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{A}(K)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} pert
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \in \mathcal{A}(K^\pert)
              be any two causally unrelated localized obserbales. Then for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})
              a DHR representation, we have on the one hand
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\rho(A B) = \rho(A) \pi_0(B)
            %p and on the other, due to the causal structure
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\rho(A B) = \rho(B A) = \pi_0(B) \rho(A)
                    \\,.
            %p
              This says that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(A) \in \pi_0(\mathcal{A}(K'))'
              and hence by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag+duality"} Haag duality
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ″
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \in \pi_0(\mathcal{A}(K))''
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
              takes values in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebras"} von Neumann algebras
              this in turn in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots = \pi_0(\mathcal{A}(K))
              and so
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              indeed factors as an endomrphism of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              followed by the vaccum representation.
          %p
            For this reason one can hope to gain some insights into the representations by studying the endomorphisms, while the set of endomorphisms has certainly more structure than that of representations: For example, endomorphisms may have inverses, and endomorphisms form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            by composition.
          %p
            First we define “unitarily equivalent” and “intertwiner” analog to the definition for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations+of+C-star+algebras"} representations of C-star algebras
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Two endomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_1, \rho_2
              are
              %strong unitarily equivalent
              if there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in \mathcal{A}
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mi ad
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mi U
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 2
                      %msup
                        %mi U
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_1 = ad(U) \rho_2 = U \rho_2 U^{-1}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ad
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ad(U)
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in \mathcal{A}
              are called
              %strong inner automorphisms
              (of
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \in \mathcal{A}
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 2
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \rho_1 = \rho_2 R
              %strong intertwiner
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %strong intertwining operator
          %p The DHR selection criterion selects representations that “look like the vaccum” on the causal complement of elements of our index set (in this case the double cones). The analog for endomorphims is this:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              An endomorphim
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              is
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localized+endomorphism"} localized
              or
              %strong localizable
              if there is a bounded open set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O} \in \mathcal{J}
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              is the identity on the algebra of the causal complement
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}^{\perp})
              = succeed "." do
                %strong
                  localized in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
          %p We have to restrict the notion of “unitarily equivalent” to a “localized” version accordingly:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              An element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{A}
              is a
              %strong local operator
              if there is a double cone K such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{M}(K)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %strong local unitary
              %strong locally unitarily equivalent
              if there is a unitary as in the definition of unitarily equivalent that is a local operator.
          %p
            For an endomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \rho
            be the equivalence class with respect to locally unitarily equivalence. Then we can define:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localized+endomorphism"} localized endomorphism
              is
              %strong transportable
              if for every bounded open set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O} \in \mathcal{J}
              there is a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mover
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_0 \in \hat \rho
              that is localized in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
          %p
            Transportable endomorphisms naturally form the objects of a category, the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/DHR+category"} DHR category
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#general General
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong transportable endomorphisms are compatible with the net structure
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              be a transportable endomorphism that is localized in the double cone
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo ⊃
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \supset K_0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(K)
              to itself.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong intrinsic characterization of admissible representations
            %p
              A representation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              is admissible iff there is a transportable endomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              is unitarily equivalent to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi ρ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0 \circ \rho
          %p Reference: This is theorem 2.1.3 in the book by Baumgärtel.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              %strong stability of admissible representations
            %p (i) finite direct sums of admissible representations are admissible.
            %p (ii) subrepresentations of admissible representations are admissible.
          %p For transportable endomorphisms we get even more:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p
              %strong products
              The product, i.e. the concatenation, of transportable endomorphisms is a transportable endomorphism.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_5 Theorem
            %p
              %strong product of causally disjoint localized endomorphisms is commutative
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_1, \rho_2
              be transportable endomorphisms localized in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_1, K_2
              respectivley with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⊥
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_1 \perp K_2
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_1 \rho_2 = \rho_2 \rho_1
          %p
            Note that the product of equivalenc classes is well defined, and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi ρ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi ρ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ρ
                          %mn 1
                        %msub
                          %mi ρ
                          %mn 2
                      %mo ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \rho_1 \hat \rho_2 = \widehat{\rho_1\rho_2}
          %h3#in_terms_of_local_net_cohomology In terms of local net cohomology
          %p
            The DHR superselection theory has a reformulation in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+of+local+net+of+observables"} cohomology of local nets of observables
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/DHR+category"} DHR category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Doplicher-Roberts+reconstruction+theorem"} Doplicher-Roberts reconstruction theorem
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general_2 General
          %blockquote
            %p
              See also other references at
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
          %p The original articles:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergio+Doplicher"}> Sergio Doplicher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rudolf+Haag"} Rudolf Haag
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+E.+Roberts"} John E. Roberts
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fields, observables and gauge transformations I
                %strong 13
                (1969) 1–23 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01645267"}> doi:10.1007/BF01645267
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergio+Doplicher"}> Sergio Doplicher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rudolf+Haag"} Rudolf Haag
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+E.+Roberts"} John E. Roberts
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fields, observables and gauge transformations II
                %strong 15
                (1969) 173–200 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01645674"}> doi:10.1007/BF01645674
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            and related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+statistics"} particle statistics
            in view of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergio+Doplicher"}> Sergio Doplicher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rudolf+Haag"} Rudolf Haag
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+E.+Roberts"} John E. Roberts
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Local observables and particle statistics I
                %strong 23
                (1971) 199–230 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01877742"}> doi:10.1007/BF01877742
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/communications-in-mathematical-physics/volume-23/issue-3/Local-observables-and-particle-statistics-I/cmp/1103857630.full"} euclid:cmp/1103857630
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergio+Doplicher"}> Sergio Doplicher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rudolf+Haag"} Rudolf Haag
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+E.+Roberts"} John E. Roberts
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Local observables and particle statistics I
                %strong 35
                (1974) 49–85 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01646454"}> doi:10.1007/BF01646454
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Of particular relevance (besides the original work of Doplicher and Roberts) are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Hellmut Baumgärtel:
                %em Operator algebraic Methods in Quantum Field Theory. A series of lectures.
                Akademie Verlag 1995 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:0839.46063&format=complete"}> ZMATH entry
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Halvorson"}> Hans Halvorson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+M%C3%BCger"} Michael Müger
                %em Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0602036"}> arXiv:math-ph/0602036
                )
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+entanglement+entropy"} holographic entanglement entropy
          %ul
            %li#CHMP19
              Horacio Casini, Marina Huerta, Javier M. Magan, Diego Pontello,
              %em Entanglement entropy and superselection sectors I. Global symmetries
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.10487"}> arXiv:1905.10487
              )
          %h3#ReferencesApplications Applications
          %p
            Approaches using DHR theory to rigorously derive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fusion+category"} fusion category
